# SRG-APP-000033-AU-001610  -  CAT I
# The Central Log Server must be configured to enforce approved
# authorizations for logical access to information and system resources in
# accordance with applicable access control policies.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000033-AU-001610
group:           V-206447
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000213
control_rev5:    AC-3

contribution:    Splunk enforces logical access through roles defined in authorize.conf:
                 each role enumerates its capabilities and may scope the indexes it can
                 search, the indexes it cannot, a search filter imposed on it, and the
                 roles it may grant. Authorization is evaluated after authentication for
                 native and externally authenticated users alike, so the application's
                 share is that this role structure exists and governs access.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

retired_check:   authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] role_<roleName> present
                 reason: The stanza placeholder was repeated in the key position. The keys
                         of a role stanza are capabilities and search scope settings -
                         authorize.conf.spec:76 states them as "<capability> = enabled",
                         and this instance reports srchIndexesAllowed, srchFilter,
                         importRoles, grantableRoles and capability names such as
                         admin_all_objects. No key in a role stanza is named
                         role_something, so the condition matched nothing and reported
                         Open on every instance. That is a wrong answer, not a strict one.

external:        Which capabilities, indexes and search filters are relevant to each role,
                 and therefore whether any given assignment is approved, is fixed by the
                 organization's access control policy and the documented duties of each
                 role; that comparison is the assessor's inspection and no configuration
                 value carries it.
external:        For identities held in an external directory or SAML identity provider,
                 the role a user receives follows the group membership that provider
                 asserts, mapped by authentication.conf [roleMap_<authSettings-key>]
                 (authentication.conf.spec:351) or [roleMap_<saml-authSettings-key>]
                 (:1230); the directory service carries the membership itself and is
                 assessed under its own baseline.

manual_reason:   The benchmark check text asks whether each account's permissions are
                 relevant to its role. Relevance is fixed by the organization's access
                 control policy, and no value in authorize.conf carries it. The one thing
                 configuration can report, that roles are defined, cannot fail: this
                 instance's /opt/splunk/etc/system/default/authorize.conf defines 19
                 [role_*] stanzas, among them role_admin, role_power and role_user, so a
                 condition testing that a role exists would pass on every instance and put
                 an inert test in front of a CAT I requirement. Deciding this requirement
                 means comparing the roles the evidence lists, with their capabilities,
                 index scope and search filters, against the access control policy and the
                 duties of the accounts holding each role. That comparison is the
                 assessor's.

citation:        CHANGED for INVENTION and a verdict-altering false finding. The reviewed
                 criterion decided a high-severity access control requirement on the single
                 condition 'authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] srchIndexesAllowed non_empty'.
                 That condition fails roles that are correctly configured.
                 srchIndexesAllowed has no default (authorize.conf.spec:295), and the
                 specification provides two other ways to scope the same access:
                 srchIndexesDisallowed, which :299-301 states takes precedence over
                 srchIndexesAllowed, and srchFilter at :150. A role that restricts access
                 by exclusion or by filter, and a role that carries only interface
                 capabilities and needs no search access at all, both report the key absent
                 and would be written up as findings although nothing is wrong with them.
                 The reviewed criterion also invents the organization's list: the task rule
                 on organization-defined values says the criterion is what the product must
                 be able to express, not a value the assessor supplies, and which indexes
                 belong to which role is precisely an organization-defined list. The
                 replacement tests what configuration can decide, that access is expressed
                 through roles (authorize.conf.spec:67-78), and records the relevance
                 judgment where it is actually carried, in external_dependencies. One point
                 on evaluator semantics, which this correction depends on and which also
                 governs SRG-APP-000033-WSR-000169, SRG-APP-000291-AU-000200 and
                 SRG-APP-000292-AU-000420: conditions written against a wildcard stanza
                 name such as [role_<roleName>] or savedsearches.conf [<search name>] must
                 be evaluated existentially, satisfied when at least one stanza meets the
                 whole condition set. Evaluated universally, 'srchIndexesAllowed non_empty'
                 fails on Splunk's own shipped roles and the scheduled-notification
                 conditions fail on Splunk's own shipped saved searches, which would make
                 those criteria unsatisfiable by any instance, and a criterion nothing can
                 satisfy is a defect rather than a strict standard. No condition here reads
                 anything outside Splunk configuration.
                 That replacement condition is itself now retired, above, and this
                 requirement is decided as manual. The condition was written with the
                 stanza placeholder repeated in the key position, so it tested for a
                 capability named role_something and reported Open on every instance; and
                 the existence test it was meant to be cannot fail, because the product
                 ships role stanzas in system/default. Manual records that configuration
                 does not decide this requirement, which is what the two external
                 dependencies above already state.
finding:         Not a finding against the application: Splunk Enterprise provides the
                 roles in authorize.conf through which logical access to indexes,
                 capabilities and search results is enforced, and the evidence lists every
                 role stanza the instance holds. Whether the permissions each role carries
                 are the approved ones for the accounts holding it is decided against the
                 organization's access control policy, which is an assessor inspection.
